# WordPress runs its scheduled work from cron rather than on page loads:
# wp-config.php sets DISABLE_WP_CRON. wp-cli runs the events that are due, as
# the web user, in the PHP of the command line. The job does not request
# wp-cron.php: the loopback addresses on port 80 are the health probe of
# Keel Web, which answers 404 to everything but /keel-health.
#
# A replica is read-only and every event would fail to record that it ran,
# so the job runs only when keel does not say that the node is a replica
# (/var/lib/keel/role) and the server answers that it takes writes (0). A
# server that cannot be asked gets no run: at the first pairing of a cloud
# replica the job ran in the seconds between read_only and the copy of the
# primary, and mailed root the errors (BR2, 2026-11-06, keel 0.23.89).
# The server is asked by wp-cli, with the account of wp-config.php: www-data
# has no account of its own, the mysql client was refused, and the replica
# of a cloud pair mailed root the errors of every run (BR2, 2026-11-06).
#
# Before the first boot or keel-init has completed the site, there is no
# wp-config.php and every run mailed root "'wp-config.php' not found" (BR1,
# 2026-11-05, a machine that waited for keel-init). The job waits for the
# mark 40wordpress writes when the site is ready (WORDPRESS_READY of
# lib/wordpress.sh).
MAILTO=root
*/5 * * * * www-data [ -e /var/www/.keel-wordpress-ready ] && ! grep -qx role=replica /var/lib/keel/role 2>/dev/null && [ "$(/usr/local/bin/wp --path=/var/www/wordpress eval 'echo (int) $GLOBALS["wpdb"]->get_var("SELECT @@global.read_only");' 2>/dev/null)" = 0 ] && /usr/local/bin/wp --path=/var/www/wordpress cron event run --due-now --quiet
